{{also|helen|Helén|hælen|Hælen}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fr|Hélène}}, from {{der|en|la|Helena}}, from {{der|en|grc|Ἑλένη|}}. {{doublet|en|Elaine}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-us-Helen.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhɛlən/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛlən|s=2}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|s}}

# {{lb|en|Greek mythology}} the daughter of [[Zeus]] and [[Leda]], considered to be the most beautiful woman in the world; her abduction by [[Paris]] brought about the Trojan War.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida|I|I|text=Fools on both sides! '''Helen''' must needs be fair,<br>When with your blood you daily paint her thus.}}
# {{given name|en|female|from=Ancient Greek}}.
#* '''1928''' [[w:Agatha Christie|Agatha Christie]], ''The Mystery of the Blue Train''
#*: "Is her name Ellen or '''Helen''', Miss Viner? I thought —"<br>Miss Viner closed her eyes.<br>"I can sound my h's, dear, as well as anyone, but '''Helen''' is ''not'' a suitable name for a servant. I don't know what the mothers in the lower classes are coming to nowadays."
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|author=[[w:Oscar Hijuelos|Oscar Hijuelos]]|title=The Fourteen Sisters of Emilio Montez O'Brien|isbn=0-14-023028-9|page=6
|passage=...in 1910 she brought '''Helen''' into the world, the little female, or "''mujercita''", as her mother called all the babies, naming her after the glittery label on a facial ointment, The '''Helen''' of Troy Beauty Pomade, said to eradicate wrinkles, to soften and add a youthful glow to the user's skin - a fortuitous choice because, of all the sisters, she would be the most beautiful and, never growing old, would always possess the face of a winsome adolescent beauty.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=[[w:Deborah Crombie|Deborah Crombie]]|title=A Share in Death|publisher=HarperCollins|isbn=0060534389|page=189
|passage=Gemma followed her, thinking that '''Helen''' seemed rather an old-fashioned and elegant name for this rumpled young mother.}}

